# Select the supported connectivity architecture

## Goal

Choose the adapter strategy and document why interactive browser login is safe,
supportable, and compatible with local and remote operation.

## Required work

- Specify `protonmail-ai auth login` through a visible, normal Proton browser
  flow managed through a maintained Playwright boundary.
- Specify revocable session storage in the operating-system credential store.
- Compare browser-session access, Proton Mail Bridge IMAP/SMTP, and a direct
  Proton API adapter.
- Record account-plan requirements, platform limits, protocol coverage, and
  failure behavior.
- Keep environment-supplied credentials explicit and less recommended.
- Keep direct API work behind a separate experimental boundary.
- Define the provider-neutral port consumed by runtime composition.
- Define adapter selection, capability negotiation, and failover rules without
  ever combining partial results from different adapters silently.

## Acceptance criteria

- One accepted architecture decision names the initial route and alternatives.
- Authentication material never crosses the MCP protocol.
- No raw browser cookie, account password, or session blob crosses MCP.
- Interactive login uses Proton's ordinary visible login flow, including user
  completion of 2FA or challenges, rather than credential replay.
- The decision maps every planned capability to supported protocol evidence or
  an explicit deferral.
- The accepted primary route can support a normal Proton account without making
  Bridge installation or a paid Bridge-compatible plan an unstated requirement.

## Evidence

- `docs/architecture/connectivity.md`
- Provider-port tests using synthetic data
